WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.120
I hate that colour.

00:00:10.160 --> 00:00:13.240
But why do think they're so popular?

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.760
Cos they fit on a piece of bread.

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.040
That's right.

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:15.880
I mean, it's that...

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:18.280
It's that square shape, isn't it?

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:21.600
See if you can find a bug in there.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:22.040
Bug free?

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.240
No.

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:25.280
Insect free?

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:31.840
Brilliant.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.240
Well, I'm going to reveal the secrets behind supermarket food, by making the ingredients that go into a sandwich.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.280
If you've ever baked your own bread, you probably prefer this to the supermarket bread.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:02.040
But the problem with this stuff is that it goes rock hard in a day or so, while the supermarket bread...

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:05.680
Well, that stays soft for ages.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:11.800
'So, what have they done to supermarket bread to make it stay softer than home-baked bread?

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:16.280
'To reveal what they're really doing to our food, I've come up with a surprising idea.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:20.360
'I've set up my own food factory inside this barn.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:25.440
'So, it's the battle of the breads.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.400
'Homemade versus factory-made.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.840
'Rock hard versus stay soft.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:37.440
'I prefer making homemade bread, 'but it doesn't stay soft for ages like supermarket bread does.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:40.040
'I'm going to try and discover why.'

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.880
Now, making bread is fairly easy, because you need very few ingredients.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:51.280
For home baked bread you only need flour, water and yeast.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:57.840
'When you add water to flour, it gets sticky and stretchy.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.480
'It's this change which makes bread possible.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.680
'Working the dough makes it stretchier.'

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.200
Look at that.

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:09.199
And that's formed by me pushing the dough backwards and forwards and being quite rough with it.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.040
That's done.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:22.720
Now what I'm going to do is pop it in this bowl here and let that rise.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:29.960
'Experts decided they could reinvent bread.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.520
'They could make it quicker and stay fresh longer.'

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:43.800
A research team in Chorleywood in Hertfordshire were trying to work on a new method of mass producing bread when they came up with a unique mixer machine.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:49.520
Now, I'm going to try and have a go at my own version of this using this bin.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:55.840
'I'm going to have to build a high power mixing machine with super strong mixing blades.

00:02:55.120 --> 00:03:03.080
'These blades will help beat lots of extra air bubbles into the dough to make it lighter.'

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.800
To get the mixing power I need, I'm going to use this beast of a drill.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:17.000
'My barn-built version of the Chorleywood machine 'will mix the dough faster and more vigorously than working the dough by hand.

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:24.280
'But supermarket bread needs more than high power mixing 'to make it stay soft.

00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:29.240
'It has more ingredients than home baked bread.'

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.120
This is bread improver and it really makes supermarket bread supermarket bread.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.840
In here are lots of different ingredients.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.480
And they're on the back of the packet here.

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.400
So, we've got fats and emulsifiers.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:48.480
And what they do, they make the air bubbles evenly spaced, small and airtight.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.000
And that keeps the bread nice and springy.

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:51.800
And stops it going stale.

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:53.720
'The added ingredients make a big difference.

00:03:53.640 --> 00:04:00.320
'I'm beginning to see why supermarket bread is so different to home baked bread.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:10.080
'Hopefully my machine is going to thrash the mix really vigorously... 'if the mixing blades don't get stuck in the sticky dough.'

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.560
Right, I almost feel too scared to push the button now.

00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:15.040
Here we go, nice and slow to start off with.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.640
Now it's starting to change.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:27.960
Its consistency's moving.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.040
It's gone from quite an inanimate lump of sticky goo into something very, very elastic.

00:04:38.920 --> 00:04:41.840
Wow.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:42.880
Well, I reckon that has worked a treat.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:50.160
It's really expanded and you can see where the mixing arms have thrashed it.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.960
Agh!

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:59.280
Right, this is like lifting... ..the biggest marshmallow you've ever met in your life out of a dustbin.

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.040
Well, that's exactly what it is.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:02.880
Look at that.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.600
And it's so sticky and stretchy.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:10.000
'Although it's sticky, it's light, 'so it must be full of those air bubbles.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:13.040
'I can't just stick it in the oven though.

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:19.320
'Just like my handmade dough, it has to rest for an hour.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:28.240
'But handmade dough takes longer, 'because it has to be worked for a second time.'

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.120
This home making of bread isn't an instant thing.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:32.560
It does take a while.

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:38.440
'So, after resting for an hour, 'what has my supermarket bread dough turned out like?'

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:40.920
Wow, look at that!

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:43.960
It looks like a monster!

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:49.880
'There's only one thing left to do to this baby - bake it.'

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:50.400
Right.

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.000
20 minutes, half an hour.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.880
That should do it.

00:05:54.200 --> 00:06:00.600
'We'll come back to the battle of the breads 'to see how my supermarket bread turns out.'

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:13.120
'Now I'll find out what they're doing 'to the insides of my sandwich to make life so convenient.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:16.800
'We're growing tomatoes almost all year round now.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:23.880
'But how can UK growers sell them at a price that we can afford?

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:26.680
'The heating bills must be astronomical.'

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.120
Because you need extra heat to grow tomatoes out of season, it makes them more expensive.

00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:38.680
And that's one reason why the vast majority of tomatoes we buy in the UK have been imported from overseas.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:47.000
John Jones at Guy & Wright in Hertfordshire supplies two of the biggest supermarkets.

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:52.880
To get the best performance from the fruit and the plant, we need to give the plant everything that it needs.

00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:57.000
The right heat, the right humidity control.

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.720
The right feed in the slab.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:07:03.200
So, your job, your main job, is to cater for every single need of this tomato plant?

00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.280
That's it.

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:07.240
We nurture it.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:12.880
'Turning up the heating to simulate summer on this scale costs a fortune.

00:07:11.920 --> 00:07:19.080
'As the price of fuel rockets, John's tomatoes might simply become too expensive.

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:21.600
'But he's come up with an ingenious idea.

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:28.840
'He's recycling waste fruit and veg from large wholesale markets and overripe imported foods.'

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.560
What's wrong with these?

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:37.320
Well, those in particular are ones that we've just de-boxed this week.

00:07:36.360 --> 00:07:38.760
They actually came from the docks.

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.080
And they were too ripe.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:39.400
They were too ripe?

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:42.520
You see, I don't think they're ready for eating yet.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.600
'I'm staggered by how much of this waste there is.

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:47.920
Have we gone completely bananas?'

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.960
Look at all this.

00:07:48.120 --> 00:07:52.560
It's hard to believe that this is actual waste, you know?

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:56.720
These have come from overseas, the Caribbean.

00:07:55.880 --> 00:08:01.080
And because they're probably not just perfect, they're thrown out.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.640
I mean, some of them aren't even ripe yet.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:04.920
Look at these ones.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:06.640
Still green.

00:08:05.680 --> 00:08:07.640
And you open them up.

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:08.320
Look at this.

00:08:10.760 --> 00:08:14.200
Open that up... and there's nothing wrong with it whatsoever.

00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:19.480
It seems criminal.

00:08:18.760 --> 00:08:24.160
There's something seriously wrong when we're wasting this amount of food.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:26.520
And it's not just bananas.

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:31.400
But at least we can put them to some use.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:43.760
'John has come up with a way to use this wasted fruit and veg to heat his glasshouses, 'saving a fortune in fuel.'

00:08:42.919 --> 00:08:53.760
'The bananas are tipped into this giant hopper 'along with tons of other fruit and veg that would otherwise go into landfill, 'and then mashed into a pulp.'

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.560
So this is where you make a great big banana smoothie, is it?

00:08:57.760 --> 00:08:59.160
'The liquid is held in here...'

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:03.000
You wouldn't eat a spoonful of that, would you?

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:07.520
'..And then pumped into concrete tanks underground.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:15.440
'Inside the tanks, trillions of bacteria feed on the waste, 'giving off lots of highly flammable methane gas.'

00:09:14.520 --> 00:09:16.640
Wow, look at that.

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:21.560
Looks like tar from down there, doesn't it?

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:29.720
The methane gas is burnt to produce electricity, which is then used to heat all the glasshouses.

00:09:29.120 --> 00:09:38.320
By burning the methane, John is producing a lot of carbon dioxide, and here's one of John's really clever ideas.

00:09:37.560 --> 00:09:42.600
Tomatoes ripen much more quickly when there's extra carbon dioxide in the atmosphere.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:45.720
So he pumps the waste gas into the glasshouses.

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:51.400
The plants absorb the carbon dioxide and produce a lot more tomatoes.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:56.440
So, providing this micro climate, how does it affect your productivity?

00:09:55.880 --> 00:10:01.760
We can achieve in a block like this... which is an acre... almost 300 tons out of here.

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.000
And what would that have been like say 40 years ago?

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.320
60, 70.

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:05.840
Really?

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:07.360
60 or 70.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:08.880
That increase?

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:15.600
So, I'm really shocked to see the amount of rejected fruit and veg which turns up here every week.

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:22.680
But I suppose, thanks to John's ingenuity, it's not wasted.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:29.800
It's the battle of the breads.

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:38.840
Why does supermarket bread stay soft, while home baked bread goes stale?

00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:50.680
The secret is the super speedy mixing, which traps more air in the dough and the added ingredients which helps stop the bread drying out.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:11:00.280
My supermarket bread is ready to come out of the oven, but the hand-mixed dough still has to rest for another half an hour.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:00.960
Right, I'm quite excited about this.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.480
Let's have a look.

00:11:01.640 --> 00:11:03.600
Oh, my word!

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:07.400
I think I've created a monster.

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:10.040
Look at this!

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.080
Do you know what?

00:11:10.120 --> 00:11:13.520
I don't know what to say.

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:17.440
I don't know whether to be proud or ashamed.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:17.680
Look at it.

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:18.080
Right.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:23.360
It seems nice and... nice and cooked.

00:11:23.440 --> 00:11:26.480
There, look at that.

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:30.640
Well, the bottom half looks all right.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:33.480
The top half looks like an abomination.

00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:42.200
'The dough was so deceptively light and springy, 'I think I put too much in the tin.'

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:45.800
But one thing for sure is that this process is incredibly quick.

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.840
I mean, these are my two loaves here.

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:49.280
They're just still proving really.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.160
This little... ha!

00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:57.960
This little monster has been mixed, it's risen and it's been baked in a fraction of the time.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.480
'If it looks like this outside, what on earth does it look like inside?

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:05.680
Oh, yeah, actually no.

00:12:04.920 --> 00:12:07.320
That's not too bad at all.

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:08.800
Look at that.

00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.640
That's a really good network of bubbles.

00:12:11.720 --> 00:12:16.320
Well, I reckon doesn't matter if it's a bit ugly, that is a success.

00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.080
Do you know what?

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:24.920
That is a really good loaf.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:32.040
Luckily I mixed so much dough, I actually filled two tins.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:34.680
But I put a lot less in this one.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:39.040
Smaller sister, much better looking.

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:41.800
There we are.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:42.440
Look at that.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:42.840
That's more like it.

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.400
Perfect.

00:12:44.760 --> 00:12:48.120
Not too sure about the ugly sister though.

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:54.880
Soft and sliced, just like the supermarket.

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:59.720
There you go, my supermarket loaf.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:06.160
'But mine won't really be as good as the supermarket bread unless it stays soft for over a week.

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.120
'So here comes the real test.'

00:13:07.360 --> 00:13:11.360
Nine days ago, I made supermarket bread.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:12.200
Now, let's see how it's got on.

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:17.680
Now, this is real supermarket bread which was bought on the same day.

00:13:16.760 --> 00:13:18.280
Let's have a look.

00:13:18.200 --> 00:13:21.560
Softish.

00:13:20.640 --> 00:13:22.720
Starting to go off, that.

00:13:22.040 --> 00:13:26.160
This is my version of supermarket bread.

00:13:25.280 --> 00:13:26.840
Let's have a look.

00:13:26.840 --> 00:13:28.000
Let's just take one at random.

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:31.440
Smells fresh, still soft.

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.320
But look at this.

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:38.480
This is the bread made to a traditional farmhouse recipe.

00:13:37.640 --> 00:13:40.120
Hard as a rock.

00:13:42.080 --> 00:13:44.960
'So it's the battle of the breads.

00:13:44.240 --> 00:13:50.120
'Pick your winner - home baked bread or supermarket bread?'

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:02.440
Right, that's the bread sorted.

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:05.320
But what are they doing to the insides of our sandwiches?

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:14.840
Now I've heard that salad leaves are treated with chemicals and sprayed with tons of pesticides to make sure we never get a bug in our bagged salad.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:19.320
Well, I wanted to find out if this was really true.

00:14:18.680 --> 00:14:25.040
Every summer, this salad producer wages daily war against enemy bugs.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:32.760
Defending their 400-mile-long front demands a military campaign.

00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:40.440
Week in week out, Vitacress supply a million and a half packs of tender leaves to supermarkets.

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:48.480
They deploy intelligence gathering, covert operations and some heavy armaments to stop the invading bugs from infiltrating their bags.

00:14:49.160 --> 00:14:58.800
Now, if you're a pest that likes eating salad, then this is gourmet heaven.

00:14:57.920 --> 00:15:03.880
But how do you get the pests off when it comes to harvest time?

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:06.920
The battle commences as soon as the lettuce seed is sown.

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:09.960
These simple flags signal the start of hostilities.

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:16.960
These sticky yellow squares are a low-tech, high-tack solution to trapping insect problems.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:22.800
These camouflaged bat boxes house a squadron of airborne allies.

00:15:22.040 --> 00:15:25.800
During their nightly sorties every enlisted bat can pick off up to 3,000 insects.

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:31.440
Sam is commander of field operations.

00:15:30.640 --> 00:15:41.280
His job is to stop enemy bugs and insects from destroying the crop, but he must spray as little pesticide as possible.

00:15:40.360 --> 00:15:46.520
After inspection, he orders me to wear protective headgear.

00:15:45.600 --> 00:15:47.120
Here, have a hat.

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:47.320
A present?

00:15:46.400 --> 00:15:50.040
It's to stop contamination getting into our crops.

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:54.640
Sam enlists undercover reinforcements.

00:15:55.320 --> 00:16:03.280
As long as he sows wild flower seeds, they will willingly set up camp and eat other pests for their rations.

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:03.720
This is a vetch plant.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:13.960
These are attracting beneficial insects such as ladybirds and your hover flies, which eat aphids and keep them out of our crops.

00:16:13.000 --> 00:16:14.320
So you rely on nature to... to help you out basically?

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:17.160
Where possible we'll use nature and natural ways of controlling pests.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:23.440
They do use pesticides here, but this arsenal of natural weapons reduces the need for spraying.

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:32.160
When the salad crop is ready for bagging, the really heavy artillery comes out.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:38.640
Heavy metal weaponry announces the arrival of the harvester.

00:16:37.840 --> 00:16:44.120
The clanking chains shake the leaves and frighten off the enemy.

00:16:43.160 --> 00:16:51.240
Now, as the machine moves along the rows, it pushes all the leaves of the lettuce backwards and forwards.

00:16:50.400 --> 00:16:55.480
And that drives all those insect pests away.

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:57.360
But Sam's work isn't done yet.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:59.040
He's determined to make sure none of the bugs end up in a bag.

00:16:58.280 --> 00:17:07.560
But when you're harvesting 50,000 kilos a day, some of the blighters are bound to slip through.

00:17:06.839 --> 00:17:10.920
The next stage, as you see here, is the second bouncing belt.

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:19.480
This bouncing belt has holes that are small enough to let the leaf carry on going up, but large enough to let insects fall through.

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:21.319
This is an impressive defensive display.

00:17:20.599 --> 00:17:26.760
But I can't believe a really determined bug hanging onto its lunch won't get through.

00:17:25.880 --> 00:17:30.920
But Sam reassures me there's even more in store.

00:17:30.760 --> 00:17:39.280
The secret to keeping freshly cut leaves from wilting is to get them really cold and fast.

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:47.000
Production manager Steve says target temperature is one degree C within the hour, but even the extreme cold doesn't eliminate the bugs.

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:53.400
The colder we make the leaves, the colder the insects are that have got caught up in the harvest.

00:17:53.600 --> 00:17:55.400
So they effectively go to sleep.

00:17:54.880 --> 00:17:57.560
They're lying there passively on or amongst the leaves.

00:17:57.040 --> 00:18:00.280
Not moving, they're harder to spot.

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:03.840
So, I mean, what's the largest creature that's actually come through here?

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:08.600
Well, every so often we'll get a frog caught up in the harvest.

00:18:07.840 --> 00:18:13.120
Remarkably they come through the vacuum process unharmed, hopping at the other end.

00:18:12.160 --> 00:18:18.280
Well, the last thing you want is to open a bag of salad and a frog leaps out.

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.760
Exactly.

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:21.120
The final push is at the packing plant.

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:25.320
Even here a sleepy slug or a brave bug might still hope to wake up inside a bag.

00:18:24.440 --> 00:18:29.520
The leaves are immediately subjected to high-tech interrogation.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:36.600
So the leaves have been unloaded and they're cascading over this conveyer belt into this machine.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:38.440
What does this bit of kit do?

00:18:37.680 --> 00:18:48.360
Basically it's using state of the art cameras and software equipment, taking a photo 4,000 times a second of the leaves.

00:18:47.480 --> 00:18:49.120
4,000 times a second it takes a snap?

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:50.160
Yeah.

00:18:49.640 --> 00:18:56.960
They're all then fed into the computer system, looking for defects, looking for insects or any problems with the leaf.

00:18:56.920 --> 00:19:05.400
If it spots what it sees is a insect or a defect, it sends a blast of air to the right place on the conveyer belt to eject that defect or leaf.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:16.800
Bagged salad got a bad reputation when it was revealed that some producers wash it in mild bleach.

00:19:16.240 --> 00:19:22.120
But here the leaves are pampered in a bath of pure spring water.

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:30.160
We have a sub merger wheel, which is a mesh wheel that pushes the leaves under the water, and gives a chances for any buoyant insects to come up and be separated.

00:19:29.280 --> 00:19:31.880
So, the washing process is the final process.

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:33.040
Yeah.

00:19:32.120 --> 00:19:35.480
That's the last in a whole series of what we call hurdles really.

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:40.520
So it's very unlikely that anything will have got by all the lines of defences we erect.

00:19:40.000 --> 00:19:53.680
With so many hurdles to jump, and so many packs, I calculate you'd have to buy a bag a week for 1,442 years before you'd be likely to find a bug in your bag.

00:19:53.120 --> 00:19:57.280
The last stage of production is to get the salad leaves in the bags.

00:19:57.120 --> 00:19:59.880
So, this is the final process.

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:01.880
They're about to go off to the supermarket?

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:02.800
Yeah, basically.

00:20:02.160 --> 00:20:06.920
The leaves have been spun to get rid of the surplus water.

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:08.040
They're pre-weighed up above us.

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:09.920
They're dropping down that tube.

00:20:10.600 --> 00:20:13.760
And as they fall they're being dropped into preformed bags.

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:14.840
Final product going by on the conveyer.

00:20:14.680 --> 00:20:18.320
So each bag should be totally bug free?

00:20:17.480 --> 00:20:19.280
Yup.

00:20:18.640 --> 00:20:20.800
That's what we aim to achieve.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:23.280
Well, fingers crossed, eh?

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:27.800
'So, if a bug does make it all the way into your bag, don't be cross.

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:29.360
'Congratulate him!'

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:31.880
No bugs.

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:44.160
Walk down the cheese aisle of any supermarket and you'll find everything from blocks of cheese like this to this stuff.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:50.200
But what is the difference between normal cheese and processed cheese?

00:20:49.320 --> 00:20:52.080
Two different types of cheese here.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:53.480
What are the differences between the two?

00:20:52.880 --> 00:20:57.000
It's maybe the flavour.

00:20:56.120 --> 00:20:57.520
Right.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.040
What about this one?

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:00.680
See, if I told you that was only 60% cheese...

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:03.120
Yeah?

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:06.920
..What do you think the rest of the 40% is made up of?

00:21:05.960 --> 00:21:08.120
I don't know.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:09.880
Who knows?

00:21:08.960 --> 00:21:10.160
Yes.

00:21:09.200 --> 00:21:11.680
Two bits of cheese here, OK?

00:21:10.720 --> 00:21:13.560
Tell me what the differences are between them and...

00:21:12.640 --> 00:21:15.240
Processed.

00:21:14.280 --> 00:21:15.640
Not processed.

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:16.920
Processed.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:17.400
Not processed.

00:21:16.440 --> 00:21:22.880
So, if I told you that that is only 60% cheese, what else do you reckon's inside it?

00:21:22.440 --> 00:21:26.680
Water.

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:27.200
Water, yeah, yeah.

00:21:26.480 --> 00:21:28.680
I haven't got a clue.

00:21:27.920 --> 00:21:29.080
Salt.

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:30.200
Salt, yeah.

00:21:29.400 --> 00:21:31.800
Don't really know.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:40.160
To discover the difference between normal cheese and processed cheese, I'm going to make both here in the barn.

00:21:39.400 --> 00:21:43.720
And I'll start with normal cheese.

00:21:42.920 --> 00:21:51.040
Right, what I've got here is a glass of warm milk, but watch what happens when I put some lemon juice in it.

00:21:51.640 --> 00:21:55.640
Now, give that a little stir.

00:21:57.480 --> 00:21:59.800
Pass that through a sieve.

00:21:59.320 --> 00:22:07.800
Now, the lemon juice has split this milk into two separate parts.

00:22:06.920 --> 00:22:15.920
All that in there, that's the whey, and what's left behind in my sieve are all the curds.

00:22:15.120 --> 00:22:17.040
That's what forms cheese.

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:18.960
And that's normal cheese.

00:22:18.120 --> 00:22:25.640
The acid in the lemon juice causes the solids in the milk to separate from the liquid.

00:22:25.360 --> 00:22:30.840
In the factory they make cheese in a big vat.

00:22:30.240 --> 00:22:35.040
So, I've knocked up my own version using this heated tank.

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:37.400
Right, stirrer goes in.

00:22:38.360 --> 00:22:42.520
Now, this milk won't turn to cheese on its own.

00:22:41.640 --> 00:22:43.480
I need some help.

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:48.360
In fact, 100 billion helpers.

00:22:47.520 --> 00:22:49.000
They're all in here.

00:22:48.160 --> 00:22:50.920
Bacteria.

00:22:50.200 --> 00:22:54.040
In the factory, they don't use lemon.

00:22:53.240 --> 00:23:00.040
They use the bacteria I've added to this milk to produce acid.

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:05.440
The acid helps separate the curds from the whey.

00:23:04.600 --> 00:23:05.800
There we go.

00:23:04.920 --> 00:23:06.880
Right.

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:10.120
Last in, some rennet, which is a coagulant to help the curds solidify.

00:23:10.320 --> 00:23:11.920
That's all in.

00:23:11.080 --> 00:23:19.400
So far, the same as all cheese is made, and after a few minutes, a remarkable change takes place.

00:23:19.160 --> 00:23:25.320
Now, when you first look at the milk it doesn't look any different, but that rennet has done it's job.

00:23:24.640 --> 00:23:26.040
And let's just try something.

00:23:25.080 --> 00:23:28.640
There we go.

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:29.680
Look at that.

00:23:30.320 --> 00:23:34.000
And what has happened, it's set.

00:23:33.360 --> 00:23:37.200
Almost like a blancmange.

00:23:36.560 --> 00:23:42.320
Now I'm going to slice the cheese, so the liquid runs out.

00:23:41.520 --> 00:23:53.040
You can really start to see you've got the big blocks, which are the curds, and that's separated from this liquid, the whey.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:55.480
Now, I've got to get rid of as much of that whey as possible.

00:24:04.480 --> 00:24:06.200
It's starting to shoot out in all different directions.

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:12.240
Now, it's starting to look like cheese.

00:24:11.440 --> 00:24:13.080
Look at that.

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:20.880
I'll just squeeze out the last of the liquid.

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:24.520
Push that down.

00:24:23.720 --> 00:24:28.080
And that's the recipe for normal cheese.

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:34.680
Now, if I was making regular cheese, I'd leave that for a month to mature, but I'm turning it into processed cheese.

00:24:33.800 --> 00:24:39.040
So, what's the different between normal cheese and processed cheese?

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:49.200
Well, they look a bit different, and they behave a bit differently when you heat them.

00:24:48.280 --> 00:24:56.640
Look, the fat doesn't seep out of the processed cheese, which is why it works well in hamburgers.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:25:02.280
So, what do they do to normal cheese to turn it into processed cheese?

00:25:01.400 --> 00:25:03.960
Processed cheese is only about 60% cheese.

00:25:04.880 --> 00:25:14.160
Now, I'm going to turn my normal cheese into processed cheese by adding the other 40% of stuff the factory puts in.

00:25:13.640 --> 00:25:20.440
Mostly it's water and some milk powder to bulk it up and make it softer.

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:26.720
This is what turns normal cheese into processed cheese.

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:28.320
'Fat and water don't mix.

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:32.240
'So I'm going to add these special melting salts.'

00:25:31.280 --> 00:25:33.080
Blender on.

00:25:33.520 --> 00:25:37.480
'The melting salts will help 'lock all the extra water into my normal cheese.'

00:25:36.520 --> 00:25:39.360
But now it's time for my cheese.

00:25:42.760 --> 00:25:50.000
As I heat them all up, I hope I'll end up with a smooth paste, with all the ingredients fully mixed together.

00:25:51.440 --> 00:25:55.800
What have I done to my perfectly good cheese?

00:25:54.880 --> 00:25:58.160
I've turned it into scrambled egg.

00:25:57.200 --> 00:26:03.480
Surprisingly my normal cheese and the added water are binding together nicely.

00:26:02.600 --> 00:26:05.280
The melting salts seem to be working.

00:26:05.280 --> 00:26:08.640
Full power.

00:26:07.680 --> 00:26:08.840
Right.

00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:11.520
Something's changed here.

00:26:11.320 --> 00:26:17.720
Stirring, stirring, stirring, and then this cheese is suddenly turned to quite a thick doughy consistency.

00:26:18.880 --> 00:26:21.120
Look at that.

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:23.840
Really stringy.

00:26:25.480 --> 00:26:26.760
Right.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:31.360
I've got to act quickly now to pour this out before it goes rock hard.

00:26:32.440 --> 00:26:37.800
It's actually quite hard to get out the bowl.

00:26:40.480 --> 00:26:46.000
And this, believe it or not, is how they make processed cheese into slices.

00:26:46.040 --> 00:26:53.360
Well, it's no masterpiece, but I reckon that's as close as I'm going to get.

00:26:53.240 --> 00:26:58.720
'OK, it's a bit wrinkly, but not a bad colour.'

00:26:58.840 --> 00:27:03.600
Look at that.

00:27:03.000 --> 00:27:09.000
Now that would repair a tractor tire when it's got a puncture.

00:27:08.120 --> 00:27:10.640
Look at this stuff!

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:13.880
Look how thick it is.

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:19.240
'Some of it's as thick as tractor tires, 'but this end isn't so bad.'

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:23.440
Let's see what it tastes like.

00:27:23.080 --> 00:27:26.240
It feels like processed cheese.

00:27:25.440 --> 00:27:30.120
It doesn't taste of a lot, but I'm really proud of how thin it is.

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:31.200
Look at that!

00:27:33.120 --> 00:27:35.840
There we go.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:37.800
My processed cheese slices.

00:27:37.480 --> 00:27:42.120
Later we made up another batch to the same recipe.

00:27:41.520 --> 00:27:45.640
To see if it would pass the taste test down on my farm.

00:27:45.040 --> 00:27:48.040
I know you haven't quite eaten it yet.

00:27:47.280 --> 00:27:49.320
But did you like the cheese?

00:27:48.360 --> 00:27:50.080
It was creamy.

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:51.160
It's nice.

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:54.560
It's better than the normal processed cheese.

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:57.880
Bit rubbery for me, really.

00:27:56.960 --> 00:27:58.080
Rubbery?

00:27:57.120 --> 00:27:58.360
Rubbery.

00:27:57.520 --> 00:28:00.680
What do you think of my cheese?

00:27:59.720 --> 00:28:00.880
Yes.

00:27:59.960 --> 00:28:02.000
You thought it was good?

00:28:01.040 --> 00:28:02.920
Good.

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:03.760
What would you give me out of 10 for that cheese?

00:28:02.920 --> 00:28:05.040
I'd say about 7.

00:28:04.600 --> 00:28:06.560
Nine, ten.

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:07.720
Nine, ten!

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:08.800
Full marks?

00:28:07.840 --> 00:28:09.920
Yeah.

00:28:08.960 --> 00:28:10.640
Show me your hands.

00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:10.960
Show me your hands.

00:28:10.040 --> 00:28:11.400
All gone?

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:14.160
All gone, look.

00:28:13.200 --> 00:28:22.640
That's just some of the clever science supermarkets use so that we can buy bread once a week, have cheese slices the perfect size and enjoy tomatoes out of season.

00:28:21.800 --> 00:28:26.600
And eat salad without worrying about unwanted caterpillars.

00:28:25.720 --> 00:28:28.520
I don't think I can look at the humble sandwich in the same way again.
